<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahanadi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mahanadi is a major river of east-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, about 850 kilometres long, rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a> and flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>. Its fertile delta around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuttack">Cuttack</a> was a heartland of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> and of later Odia kingdoms, and many small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> lay along its middle course. The Hirakud Dam, completed in 1957, is among the longest earthen dams in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahanadi.jpg" alt="Mahanadi" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LIPU1995 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahanadi_from_Banki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name Mahanadi combines the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> words maha, great, and nadi, river. The river drains around 132,100 square kilometres of east-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and flows through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>. Like many seasonal Indian rivers it is a combination of mountain streams, so its precise source cannot be pinpointed. Its farthest headwaters lie in forest south of Sihawa town in Dhamtari district of Chhattisgarh, in hills that extend the Eastern Ghats.</p><p>It first flows north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raipur">Raipur</a> district, then east after the Shivnath joins it, and enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> after about half its length. Near Sambalpur it is dammed at Hirakud. It then forces its way through the Eastern Ghats by the 64-kilometre Satkosia Gorge and reaches the plain at Naraj, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuttack">Cuttack</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuttack">Cuttack</a> and Sambalpur were prominent trading places in the ancient world, and Ptolemy refers to the river as the Manada. Before the Hirakud Dam the Mahanadi was navigable from its mouth up to Arrang, but barrages have ended that.</p><p>The river was notorious for devastating floods for much of recorded history and was called the sorrow of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>. It deposits more silt than any other river of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>, and before the dam it was about a mile wide at Sambalpur. At its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> peak its discharge is almost as great as that of the much larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, but for most of the year it is a narrow channel between wide sand banks. The Hirakud Dam was the first major multipurpose river valley project after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s independence in 1947. It is the longest earthen dam in the world, measuring 26 kilometres including its dykes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Just before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuttack">Cuttack</a> the river gives off a large distributary, the Kathjori, and the city stands on the spit between the two channels. The Mahanadi proper enters the sea by several channels near Paradeep. The combined delta of its distributaries and the Brahmani is one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the valley is best known for its fertile soil and agriculture.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahanadi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tradition places the founding in the late 12th century, when a family in the service of the kings of Puri received land near the <b>Mahanadi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/athgarh-state">Athgarh State</a></li><li><blockquote>Athmallik was a forested, thinly populated state on the south bank of the <b>Mahanadi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/athmallik-state">Athmallik State</a></li><li><blockquote>Baudh was an inland state of farmland and forest on the <b>Mahanadi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/baudh-state">Baudh State</a></li><li><blockquote>Hindol lay in the forested hills north of the <b>Mahanadi</b>, in today's Dhenkanal district.</blockquote><a href="/state/hindol-state">Hindol State</a></li><li><blockquote>It lay south of the <b>Mahanadi</b>, near the gorge where the river leaves the hills for the coastal plain.</blockquote><a href="/state/khandpara-state">Khandpara State</a></li><li><blockquote>Kalinga, the coastal plain between the <b>Mahanadi</b> and Godavari rivers, appears in the Mahabharata and in Buddhist texts, which name Dantapura as an early capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kalinga">Kingdom of Kalinga</a></li></ul></section>
